WHAT IS HERE, AND WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN THE CLONE

`../code/` is a pristine clone of ScholarQABench at

    95e6fc52b0a8a0ce0a74956029991e3bb00c38b9

and `../../setup.sh --check` asserts it stays that way. Nothing of ours is
written inside it. These files are ours: derived from upstream's, changed
substantially, and maintained here.

Upstream never imports them — they are standalone scripts, invoked by path —
so moving them out of the clone costs nothing and buys the guarantee that the
benchmark code we score against is unmodified. `run_utils.py` stays beside them
because `citation_correctness_eval.py` imports it as a flat module, the way it
does upstream.

MODIFICATIONS

  - `citation_correctness_eval.py` — roughly twice its upstream length
    (+935/-200). Added: a configurable AutoAIS input-token cap with an
    out-of-memory fallback that retries at successively shorter limits, chunked
    scoring with an optional model reload per chunk (`--autoais_chunk_size`,
    `--autoais_reload_model_per_chunk`), and a per-question results dump. These
    make the full 1451- and 1308-question sets scoreable on one GPU; they do not
    change how a citation is judged.
  - `prometheus_eval.py` (+719/-163) — points at a judge served over an
    OpenAI-compatible endpoint (`--judge_model litellm_openai`,
    `--judge_base_url`) instead of loading it in-process, and splits scoring
    into the two passes `--judge_pass org_cov` and `--judge_pass relevance`.
  - `run_utils.py` (+24/-10) — retry with exponential backoff on rate limits,
    plus logging and formatting.

Upstream's `requirements.txt` became `../../envs/sqa-scoring.in`, reduced to
what these two scorers actually import and resolved for Linux; see the comments
there.

WHAT WE DID NOT CHANGE

Everything else in the clone is upstream's, untouched, and asserted to be so.
`rubrics/prometheus_rubrics_v8.json` is read straight out of it.

Our internal tree also carries changes to the ScholarQA-CS scripts
(`rubric_eval.py`, `scholarqacs_rubric.py`, `convert_answer_scholar_cs.py`,
`parse_annotations.py`). They are deliberately not here: this harness never
invokes them, and the CS split is not one of the paper's rows.
